From: ADIOS scheduler analysis
Subject: [PATCH] adios: adds #5, BFQ-inspired interactive-boost heuristic

Applies ON TOP of adios-android14-6.1-v3.2.0.patch. Same base as the
previous revision (confidence-gated ordering, auto queue-depth scaling,
self-detected degradation decay, tunable LM constants, SAGA defaults --
see that revision's own commit message for those). This adds ONE new
mechanism on top:

5. Interactive-boost heuristic (task_was_idle_and_gets_boost(), used in
   add_to_dl_tree()). Inspired by BFQ's core insight for desktop/mobile
   responsiveness -- a task that was idle and just issued I/O is likely
   interactive (foreground touch response, app resuming) and benefits
   from a temporary priority bump -- WITHOUT implementing BFQ's full
   per-process fair-queuing machinery (that's thousands of lines and a
   different architecture). Scoped down to: a small fixed 16-slot
   recency table (tgid -> last seen time) in struct adios_data, checked
   at insert time. A sync READ from a tgid whose last I/O was more than
   lm_interactive_idle_ms ago (default 100ms) -- or has no entry at all
   -- gets its deadline reduced by lm_interactive_boost_ns (default
   20ms), letting it jump the Tier-2 FIFO ahead of continuously-active
   readers. Deliberately READ-only and SYNC-only: that's the pattern of
   a UI thread blocking on a resource read, the case that actually
   shows up as jank. Async writes are untouched -- boosting them would
   fight the write-deprioritization the rest of ADIOS already does on
   purpose.

Design notes / honesty about limitations:
- Uses task_tgid_nr(current) at insert time. This assumes
  adios_insert_requests() runs in the issuing thread's own context
  (true for the normal blk-mq submit_bio path; if a given request
  reaches insert via a plug-list flush on a different thread/workqueue,
  the tgid captured could be wrong -- worst case is a missed or
  misattributed boost, not a correctness bug, since it only ever
  shifts a deadline earlier by a bounded amount).
- The 16-slot table is a hint structure, not accounting: collisions
  between unrelated tgids just mean a missed boost occasionally, never
  incorrect ordering. No claim of exactness.
- Needed one new #include (linux/sched.h, for task_tgid_nr) -- verified
  not already pulled in by this file's existing includes, added
  explicitly rather than assumed transitive.
- 100ms idle / 20ms boost are reasoned starting points, NOT validated
  by benchmark, same as every other new constant in this patch series.

NOT submitted upstream. NOT compiled/tested against a real
android14-6.1 tree by me -- the PREVIOUS revision of this patch (parts
1-4 + SAGA defaults) WAS built, flashed, and confirmed running on the
user's real device (adios_version sysfs reads 3.2.0-tunable-lm.2, and
the batch_limit auto-scale in part #2 visibly fired: read/write scaled
from the 64/128 base to 123/247, consistent 1.93x on both, implying a
real q->nr_requests of ~62 on that UFS 2.2 device). This revision's new
part (#5) has NOT yet been through that same real-build verification.
